   "source": [
    "Scalar types are pretty easy to use and understand. When you need to store a string value, it will be obvious which\n",
    "variable type to reach for. Actually, Python will just let you store the value. No need to reach for anything! Knowing\n",
    "which container type to reach for is a bit harder. When you've been writing Python for a while, you'll get a \"feel\" for\n",
    "it. Until then, make mistakes! If you use a dictionary instead of a list, that mistake will become clear at some point\n",
    "in your development cycle. Refactor the code to use the right type. It will cement the lesson in your mind.\n",
    "\n",
    "A few starter thoughts that should work until you develop a feel for it:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Sets\n",
    "    - Unordered.\n",
    "    - Automatically removes duplicates.\n",
    "    - The fastest container type.\n",
    "    - There's no easy way to reference a specific element in a set by value or position (as sets are unordered)\n",
    "- Lists\n",
    "    - Ordered.\n",
    "    - Faster than dictionaries, slower than sets.\n",
    "    - Lists can be referenced by index.\n",
    "- Dictionaries\n",
    "    - Ordered in more recent Python versions. Prior to Python 3.10 or something, order wasn't maintained.\n",
    "    - Slower than both sets and lists.\n",
    "    - If you need to reference the value later by value, this is a great way option.\n",
    "- Tuples\n",
    "    - Immutable. Once you create one, you can't alter a tuple. This makes a tuple a tricky \"container\" as you would need\n",
    "      to know all the potential values prior to creating it. It's also hard to reference items by value. What it is good\n",
    "      for is better served by other types we'll cover in later chapters. I don't reach for tuples often."
