    "# Functions\n",
    "\n",
    "Functions are excellent! They have a few benefits:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Functions make your code easier to read.** Repeating the same logic in multiple places increases the length of your\n",
    "  code and the mental energy required to understand it. Once you understand a function, you can reuse that understanding\n",
    "  when you come across it again.\n",
    "- **Functions make your code easier to maintain.** Imagine you write identical code in multiple places across your code\n",
    "  base. That's likely fine today, but future features or maintenance might not be applied to all the copies. Now, you'll\n",
    "  have __similar__ code in a few places. That's less than helpful, as __similar__ code can appear identical on first\n",
    "  glance.\n",
    "- **Functions make your code easier to debug.** If you've already seen the same 12 - 15 lines of code, you're more\n",
    "  likely to skim and miss subtle differences. When you refactor that code into a function, those subtle differences will\n",
    "  be `if / else` statements that will make the differences explicit.\n",
    "- **Functions can be used to narrate your code.** Functions and function parameters require names. These names can serve\n",
    "  as documentation. If you see `write_results_to_csv(results=some_result, csv_file=\"my_filename.csv\")`, you know what's\n",
    "  happening without even seeing the function definition.\n",
    "\n",
