   "source": [
    "My recommendation: **Use keyword arguments whenever possible.**\n",
    "\n",
    "Which of these two code snippets would you rather read:\n",
    "\n",
    "```python\n",
    "result = forier_transform(img, {\"grid_x\": 4, \"grid_y\": 6})\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```python\n",
    "result = forier_transform(\n",
    "    input_image=img,\n",
    "    transform_parameters={\"grid_x\": 4, \"grid_y\": 6}\n",
    ")\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "The second one, right?\n",
    "\n",
    "It's not just about readability. If you're calling a function from a 3rd-party library, it's possible that the order the\n",
    "positional arguments changes from one version to another. That would be called out in the release documentation, but\n",
    "we're not always referencing that documentation when we upgrade packages. Using keyword arguments insulates you from\n",
    "this. Mature 3rd-party libraries rarely make changes like this. On the other hand, the functions that **you** define may\n",
    "change significantly with each commit. The benefit of keyword arguments is especially pronounced here."
