    "# Scope\n",
    "\n",
    "When we introduced control logic functions, we introduced another shadow concept: scope. Every Python script has a\n",
    "`global` scope. A gross simplification: Every indent introduces a new nested scope. Every dedent returns us back to the\n",
    "parent scope.\n",
    "\n",
    "The `global` scope can either be modified when you're in the global scope (no indentation) or by using the `global`\n",
    "keyword to let Python know that you're referring to a global variable. **Altering global variables isn't considered a\n",
    "best practice, so we won't be doing it in this program.** I only mention it because you might come across this behavior\n",
    "as you are exploring the language."
